# Slice 334 (Codex review of 333, item 1): descriptions of functions written in code, each with the field name -- the
# second a descriptor without a body -- compose by the same rules as any Structure: the part args is composed (b takes
# d2's default 4), name and return are definitions the later operand writes, the body stays d1's (code is not glued).
# `fn: h merge(d1, d2)` names the function h (the field name of the parts is an ordinary field); the whole-Structure form
# `fn: merge(d1, d2)` takes the field name of its composition, two. d1 and d2 stay as written; neither is a function.
d1:
    name: one
    args: (int: a; int: b 1)
    return: int
    body: (return: a * 10 + b)
end: d1
d2:
    name: two
    args: (int: b 4)
    return: int
end: d2
fn: h merge(d1, d2)
fn: merge(d1, d2)
int: r1 h(1)
int: r2 two(2)
int: r3 h(1; 7)
